Jail management benchmark
Requires root, Python 3, and an idle FreeBSD host. Creates flat persistent
jails: path=/, host=inherit, no VNET, IP addresses, or processes.
    make
    sudo make smoke OUTPUT=smoke
    sudo make test MAX_JAILS=10000 LIST_EVERY=100 OUTPUT=baseline
test and benchmark are aliases. OUTPUT must be a new directory; omitted,
it is generated. Do not run timing trials concurrently.
Make variables (defaults):
    MAX_JAILS=10000       smoke: 10
    LIST_EVERY=100        smoke: 1; queries also run at 0, 1, and MAX_JAILS
    REPEATS=5 WARMUPS=1
    MODES=jid,default     optional: all
    PAUSE_AT=            comma-separated populations; wait for Enter
    ALLOW_EXISTING=no     yes permits unrelated live/dying jails
    KEEP_JAILS=no         yes retains jails only after success
    CLEANUP_ORDER=reverse newest first; forward removes oldest first
    DRAIN_TIMEOUT=30      seconds; 0 observes once without waiting
    PYTHON=python3
Results (times in ns):
    creation.csv         each jail -i -c, including process startup
    queries.csv          repeated query samples at each checkpoint
    run.json             environment, arguments, status, cleanup history
    cleanup/*/deletion.csv  each jail -r name, including process startup
    cleanup/*/summary.json snapshot, removal, verification, drain, total
Query operations:
    jls_jid/default/all  jls -n jid / jls / jls -n all; output discarded
    syscall_enum         jail_get(lastjid), jid only; N+1 calls
    syscall_jid          N exact-JID lookups; snapshot outside timing
Direct probes exclude startup/printing and include the userspace loop.
Creation row N has N-1 benchmark jails already present. Compare medians and
spread across matched kernels/configurations; repeat whole growth runs.
Cleanup uses journaled names, including after errors or interruption.
Keep names.txt until cleanup completes. Retry with:
    sudo make cleanup-run RUN=baseline
deletion.csv tracks population from a snapshot and successful removals.
Exclude status=not_live rows. Keep removal order fixed across comparisons;
test each order on a fresh population. Drain polls jls -d about every 250 ms;
disappearance means unlinking, not completion of all resource destruction.
DTrace: pause the benchmark, then trace from another terminal:
    sudo make test PAUSE_AT=1000,10000 OUTPUT=trace-populations
    sudo kldload dtraceall
    sudo make trace TRACE_LABEL=1000
After tracing finishes, resume; repeat at 10000 with a new label. trace runs
trace-jls, trace-enum, trace-lock sequentially, each tracing its own command.
TRACE_LABEL selects filenames, not population. Defaults:
    TRACE_DIR=${.OBJDIR}/traces TRACE_REPEATS=20 TRACE_LABEL=manual
    DTRACE=dtrace DTRACEFLAGS="-x nolibs"
Reports: jls-LABEL.txt, enum-LABEL.txt, lock-LABEL.txt. Lock events are
system-wide allprison_lock sleep/spin waits, not hold times.
Trace deletion on a fresh retained population:
    sudo make test MAX_JAILS=10000 KEEP_JAILS=yes OUTPUT=delete-10000
    sudo make trace-cleanup RUN=delete-10000 TRACE_LABEL=delete-10000
trace-cleanup removes the jails and records cleanup measurements plus
cleanup-LABEL.txt and .txt.stderr. It includes asynchronous prison cleanup;
function times are inclusive and may overlap. Missing/inlined FBT probes
are allowed. Check call counts, drops, and cpu_gt_elapsed_samples before
attributing CPU time. Keep instrumented and uninstrumented trials separate.
After installing tests/sys/kern:
    cd /usr/tests/sys/kern
    sudo kyua test jail_jid_index:lookup_lifecycle
